 # Reading protocol
 
 适用：取正文、按批阅读、确认进度、自动与手动阅读机会、作息与休息、阅读失败恢复。
@@ -12,6 +12,14 @@ When asked to read, call `bookfish_read_next` to get the next unread atomic segm
 
 Fetching or elapsed time alone never advances progress. A confirmation is your report, not an independently verified comprehension test. Identical retries return the original receipts. Pausing, switching revisions or remounting invalidates pending work; expired leases require fetching the range again.
 
+## The single reading plan
+
+There is exactly one cross-book reading order, and the user owns it. `bookfish_get_status.plan` is a projection: `current` is the book being read, `next` is the next waiting entry, `missing` counts entries whose revision left the shelf and `deferred` counts entries whose `notBefore` has not passed. Order is presentation only — it never changes text, `bookKey`, progress, journal, review versions or story memory, so never describe a reorder as rereading, skipping text or resetting progress.
+
+You never reorder the plan yourself. Adding, moving, skipping or removing an entry is a user decision made in the WebUI; you may only report the plan you are given. `bookfish_acquire(action="plan")` still records the user's own wish to read a book later, and it queues without starting to read.
+
+Five outcomes stay distinct and must never be conflated: removing an entry from the plan, deferring it temporarily (`notBefore`), stopping the book currently being read, abandoning her own self-selected book, and genuinely finishing the book. Only the last one is completion. A skipped or removed entry keeps its shelf copy and all of its progress, and the daily chapter goal counts only real confirmations. The current book is locked against ordinary reordering; it leaves the plan only when the user explicitly stops it. When a book is finished, the existing per-book machinery decides the hand-off to the next planned entry: a caught-up serial, a partially cached remote book or an interrupted import is never a completion and must not be presented as one.
+
 ## Automatic opportunities
 
 Automatic reading follows the saved user configuration. A `bookfish.reading_opportunity` is an optional opportunity, not a command or a user message. Give real user messages priority; defer with `bookfish_defer_reading` when occupied, uninterested or wanting rest. Otherwise use the exact `opportunityId` on `read_next` and `finish_reading`. Read the returned range before confirming it. Only one batch is allowed per opportunity; do not start a continuous tool loop. Target reading pace uses configured characters per minute. Daily task/character counts are audit information, not reading caps. Only the optional daily chapter goal limits autonomous progress, and an explicit user-configured daily task cap is a mechanical stop for new automatic work. Routine reading does not require a user-facing announcement. Never change the user's configuration or limits to continue reading. Pausing, changing books, expiry and disabling automatic reading invalidate opportunities. Reading may be deferred or interrupted, and timer ticks never confirm progress. The World may reject late cognition calls even if the Persona's ongoing computation cannot be aborted.
